{
  "dom_id": "step-q97178",
  "name": "Question 16",
  "problem": "Given $X\u223c\\text{Geom}(0.4),$ compute $P(X\\ge 4)$ rounded to $3$ decimal places.",
  "worked_solution": "If $X\u223c\\text{Geom}(p),$ then $X$ has the following probability mass function:\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}(1-p)^{x-1}p, & x=1,2,3,\u2026 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, $X\u223c\\text{Geom}(0.4),$ so the distribution of $X$ in this case is\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}{(0.6)}^{x-1}(0.4), & x=1,2,3\u2026 \\\\ 0, & \\text{otherwise}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThere are infinitely many values of $X$ such that $X\\ge 4.$ However, we can simplify the computation by using the complement instead:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 4) & =1-P(X<4)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the complement, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<4) & =P(X\\in {1,2,3}) \\\\  & =P(X=1)+P(X=2)+P(X=3) \\\\  & =f(1)+f(2)+f(3) \\\\  & ={(0.6)}^{0}(0.4)+{(0.6)}^{1}(0.4)+{(0.6)}^{2}(0.4) \\\\  & =0.784.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\ge 4) & =1-P(X<4) \\\\  & =1-0.784 \\\\  & =0.216.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
